\description{
A style session owns a tree of explicitly keyed elements for one compiled
stylesheet and remembers the computed styles it has produced. It is an
optional, faster way to compute styles for a tree that is queried many times
and changed a little at a time (an interactive application). The stateless
functions \code{\link[=ink_match]{ink_match()}}, \code{\link[=ink_compute]{ink_compute()}} and \code{\link[=ink_explain]{ink_explain()}} keep working
without sessions and give the same results.
}
\section{Identity}{
 Nodes are identified by a \strong{key}, a string you choose
(\code{"save-button"}). The key is a session-local runtime identity and is
unrelated to the CSS \code{id} that selectors like \verb{#save} match: several nodes
may share a CSS \code{id}, and the key of a node never matters to selectors. A
session never guesses identity from an element's contents or position.
}

\section{Lifecycle}{

\enumerate{
\item \code{ink_session(sheet)} creates an empty session. The stylesheet's selectors
are prepared once.
\item \code{ink_session_set()} adds a node or updates one.
\item \code{ink_session_compute()} returns its computed style, computing only what is
not already cached.
\item \code{ink_session_remove()} removes a node and everything below it.
}

A session is a mutable object (an environment): copying the variable does not
copy the session. Two sessions made from the same stylesheet are completely
independent, and the stylesheet itself is never modified.
}

\section{Adding and updating nodes}{

\code{ink_session_set()} is an \emph{upsert}. For a new key, \code{type} is required and the
other arguments default to "none" (\code{id = NULL}, no classes or states, no
parent, i.e. a root). For an existing key, only the arguments you supply
change; omitted arguments keep their value, while \code{id = NULL},
\code{classes = character()}, \code{states = character()} and \code{parent_key = NULL}
reset a field explicitly. Setting a node to what it already is does nothing.
Attributes follow the rules of \code{\link[=ink_element]{ink_element()}}. A parent must already be in
the session, and cannot be the node or one of its descendants.

Every change is checked before anything is modified: a call that signals an
\code{ink_session_error} leaves the session exactly as it was.
}

\section{Cache and invalidation}{

Computed styles are cached per node, together with the selector-matching state
that the node's children continue from. Because descendant selectors
(\verb{.active Button}) and inheritance (\code{color}) make a node's style depend on its
ancestors, a change invalidates conservatively:
\itemize{
\item Changing a node (type, id, classes, states or parent) drops the cached
styles of that node \strong{and all its descendants}.
\item Moving a node to a new parent (reparenting) is such a change: the moved
subtree is recomputed under its new ancestors.
\item Removing a node drops its whole subtree.
\item Ancestors, siblings and unrelated nodes keep their caches.
\item Computing a node reuses the nearest valid cached ancestor and computes only
the nodes below it.
}

No finer dependency tracking (per property, per selector) is attempted.
